WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.420
In this video, you find an unhealthy virtual machine in SUSE Observability, check its metrics, and open the monitor behind its health state.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.130
Open the main menu.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.520
Expand SUSE Virtualization.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.680
Select Virtual machines.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:26.030
This VM shows Deviating. It runs on a host of the harvester-north cluster.

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:33.730
To the right, the CPU Usage, Memory Usage, and Disk Usage columns show current metrics for each VM.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:37.380
Open the Deviating VM.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:44.810
On the Highlights page, the Monitors card shows which check failed. Here, it is VM disk filling up.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:50.550
Before you open the monitor, select Metrics to see the VM's charts.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:01:01.120
Guest shows filesystem usage inside the VM, from the QEMU guest agent. Other groups cover network, storage, memory, and CPU.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.020
Return to Highlights.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:09.100
Select the VM disk filling up monitor.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:17.970
The panel explains the check. It fires when the fullest writable filesystem in the VM is more than eighty percent used.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:26.300
The triggering evidence plots that metric against its threshold. The health state shows Deviating since the trigger time.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:36.580
Below, the monitor links a remediation guide with suggested next steps. Review them against your own environment before you act.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.740
Close the panel when you are done.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:48.236
You traced an unhealthy virtual machine to the monitor behind its state. Next, you right-size virtual machines.
